WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.667
(trumpet music)

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:13.490
- Morning, ladies and gentlemen.

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:16.200
On behalf of the
president Yale University,

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.580
the commanding officer Yale University,

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:21.140
Naval Reserve Officers' Training Corps,

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.980
and commander Yale University

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.570
Air Force Reserve
Officers' Training Corps,

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:28.510
welcome to the annual President's Review.

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:29.980
We specifically acknowledge

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:31.900
and thank representatives in attendance

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:33.860
from Fairfield University,

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:35.660
Quinnipiac University,

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:37.470
Sacred Heart University,

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:40.120
Southern Connecticut State University,

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.570
Western Connecticut State University,

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:44.570
and the University of New Haven.

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:45.470
To you,

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:47.600
and to all distinguished guests,

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.400
families, and friends, welcome.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.260
Today's music is being provided by

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:55.700
the Navy Band Northeast
Trident Brass Quintet

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:58.163
from Naval Station Newport, Rhode Island.

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:00.860
Today's review is being conducted

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:02.640
by the midshipmen and cadets

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.773
of the Yale University ROTC.

00:01:13.882 --> 00:01:15.530
(murmurs)

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:16.363
- Forward.

00:01:19.355 --> 00:01:22.321
(murmurs)

00:01:22.321 --> 00:01:23.154
Sword.

00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:26.826
(murmurs)

00:01:28.608 --> 00:01:30.870
- Present day review have their basis

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.390
in both history and tradition.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:34.970
The mass formation of troops

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:37.340
on one long line at close interval

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:39.610
made possible the massing of firepower

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:42.740
from muzzle loaded muskets of yesterday.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:45.870
In those early days, the
line of battle was just that,

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:47.600
a line of two or three ranks

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:48.620
and looked much like

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:51.070
the parade formation you will see today.

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:53.447
The adjacent forms the line for battle.

00:01:55.329 --> 00:01:57.496
(murmurs)

00:01:58.432 --> 00:02:01.099
(trumpet music)

00:02:02.978 --> 00:02:04.895
- Battalion, attention.

00:02:06.216 --> 00:02:07.242
(murmurs)

00:02:07.242 --> 00:02:08.242
- Attention.

00:02:09.112 --> 00:02:11.279
(murmurs)

00:02:14.969 --> 00:02:17.636
(trumpet music)

00:02:25.841 --> 00:02:28.258
(band music)

00:03:06.518 --> 00:03:09.268
(all conversing)

00:03:10.885 --> 00:03:13.052
(murmurs)

00:03:24.029 --> 00:03:25.854
- Proceed at war.

00:03:25.854 --> 00:03:28.021
(murmurs)

00:03:45.104 --> 00:03:49.971
- Battalion (murmurs)

00:03:54.204 --> 00:03:55.454
Proceed to war,

00:03:56.537 --> 00:03:57.787
take your post.

00:04:04.486 --> 00:04:06.569
- Battalion, order march.

00:04:12.039 --> 00:04:14.206
(murmurs)

00:04:39.835 --> 00:04:44.535
Battalion (murmurs)

00:04:47.075 --> 00:04:47.908
swords.

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:53.830
- Ladies and gentlemen,

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:56.543
please rise for the arrival
of the official party.

00:05:03.544 --> 00:05:06.127
(bell ringing)

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:11.970
Yale University Air Force
Reserve Officer Training Corps

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:15.053
Unit Detachment 009 arriving.

00:05:21.996 --> 00:05:24.579
(bell ringing)

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:27.770
Yale University,

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:31.513
Naval Reserve Officers
Training Corps unit arriving.

00:05:37.559 --> 00:05:39.309
- Staff order swords.

00:05:41.895 --> 00:05:46.595
Battalion (murmurs)

00:05:49.311 --> 00:05:51.894
(bell ringing)

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:56.190
- Yale University arriving.

00:06:15.167 --> 00:06:16.917
- Staff carry swords.

00:06:19.332 --> 00:06:21.499
(murmurs)

00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:28.370
- [Captain May] Ladies and
gentlemen please remain standing

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:30.220
for the posting of the colors,

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:31.812
the national anthem,

00:06:31.812 --> 00:06:33.395
and the invocation.

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:36.103
- Forward march.

00:06:38.092 --> 00:06:40.509
(band music)

00:06:58.732 --> 00:06:59.565
(murmurs)

00:06:59.565 --> 00:07:00.398
swords.

00:07:07.093 --> 00:07:10.426
(national anthem music)

00:08:21.781 --> 00:08:23.531
Staff forward swords.

00:08:25.549 --> 00:08:27.299
Battalion order arms.

00:08:32.199 --> 00:08:35.071
(murmurs)

00:08:35.071 --> 00:08:37.488
(band music)

00:08:57.438 --> 00:08:59.605
(murmurs)

00:09:13.594 --> 00:09:14.427
Order.

00:09:15.559 --> 00:09:16.392
Color.

00:09:19.527 --> 00:09:21.694
(murmurs)

00:09:27.767 --> 00:09:31.832
- Battalion (murmurs)

00:09:31.832 --> 00:09:34.582
(all conversing)

00:09:39.742 --> 00:09:41.160
- [Captain May] Chaplain Sharon Kugler,

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:44.493
Yale University chaplain
will now give the invocation.

00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:50.960
- I invite you to join me
in the spirit of prayer.

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:55.030
Almighty God, source of all truth,

00:09:55.030 --> 00:09:56.530
here we stand

00:09:56.530 --> 00:09:58.570
humbled by the breadth of service

00:09:58.570 --> 00:10:02.230
both past and present in our midst.

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:04.820
We stand proud of what
has been made possible

00:10:04.820 --> 00:10:06.720
because we have committed ourselves

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:09.840
to making our world a better place

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:11.870
through our willing spirits,

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:13.290
able bodies,

00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:15.960
and courageous hearts.

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:19.930
Although today is a
celebration and rightly so,

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:22.380
we remember those who
have given their lives

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:24.660
in service to their country,

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:28.150
and we hold close those
who are currently deployed

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:31.250
and ask that your gentle hand protect them

00:10:31.250 --> 00:10:33.533
and guide them safely home.

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:38.680
Now may our hearts move from
gratitude to inspiration

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:41.530
as your divine spirit empowers us

00:10:41.530 --> 00:10:45.120
with a renewed vision and purpose.

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:49.750
Let us begin this review
lifted by splendor,

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:52.340
ignited by camaraderie,

00:10:52.340 --> 00:10:56.360
together in our many voices,
strengths and faiths,

00:10:56.360 --> 00:11:00.513
and as your loving people we say Amen.

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:03.233
- Please be seated.

00:11:06.140 --> 00:11:09.060
The Pass in Review has several purposes.

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:10.690
First it serves to honor

00:11:10.690 --> 00:11:13.010
and thank the leadership
of the universities

00:11:13.010 --> 00:11:15.620
that host and support our units.

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:18.150
This event also serves as an opportunity

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:20.270
to publicly recognize the achievements

00:11:20.270 --> 00:11:23.410
of the midshipmen and
cadets you see here today,

00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:27.070
who have distinguished themselves
during the academic year.

00:11:27.070 --> 00:11:28.380
Ladies and gentlemen,

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:29.570
Commander,

00:11:29.570 --> 00:11:31.090
Yale University,

00:11:31.090 --> 00:11:33.010
Naval ROTC,

00:11:33.010 --> 00:11:34.283
Captain Withrow.

00:11:37.764 --> 00:11:40.764
(audience clapping)

00:11:49.803 --> 00:11:51.803
- Good morning everyone.

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:00.650
Thank you, Captain May.

00:12:00.650 --> 00:12:02.700
Good morning and welcome to our first

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:04.080
in-person President's Review

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:08.030
and the first military Pass
in Review since April of 2019.

00:12:08.030 --> 00:12:10.400
It's hard to believe it's been that long.

00:12:10.400 --> 00:12:13.620
Colonel George Granholm of Air Force ROTC

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:15.930
and I are proud to recognize
our cadets and midshipmen

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:18.150
for distinguished performance,

00:12:18.150 --> 00:12:21.400
to recognize our graduating class of 2022,

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:24.100
who'll be commissioning
in just a few short weeks.

00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:26.550
And to thank the many
family and friends of ROTC.

00:12:27.730 --> 00:12:30.450
I'm also excited that for the
first time in several years

00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:33.900
those Yales who are part
of the Army ROTC program

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:35.340
at the University of New Haven,

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:36.693
are able to join us today.

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:39.920
Before I introduce our host,

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:42.370
I want to make some key,

00:12:42.370 --> 00:12:46.220
I wanna recognize some key
players in today's ceremony.

00:12:46.220 --> 00:12:49.840
First off we have Midshipmen
Second Class Aaron (murmurs)

00:12:49.840 --> 00:12:51.590
the Midshipmen officer in charge,

00:12:51.590 --> 00:12:53.840
responsible for planning this grand event,

00:12:53.840 --> 00:12:55.490
and assisting from the Air Force side

00:12:55.490 --> 00:12:57.863
Cadet Fourth Class Shannon Palm.

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:02.100
My executive officer Commander Scott Ryan,

00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:04.240
First Sergeant Roberto Portell,

00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:06.370
master trainer and enforcer

00:13:06.370 --> 00:13:08.373
of military drill and customs.

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:11.750
Let me say again, enforcer.

00:13:11.750 --> 00:13:13.550
Tech Sergeant Patricia (murmurs)

00:13:13.550 --> 00:13:16.070
Master Sergeant Shaniqua Hunt,

00:13:16.070 --> 00:13:18.170
Miss Liz Villadaki,

00:13:18.170 --> 00:13:20.520
master administrator coordinator,

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:21.830
ghost email writer,

00:13:21.830 --> 00:13:22.870
et cetera,

00:13:22.870 --> 00:13:24.270
et cetera,

00:13:24.270 --> 00:13:25.710
et cetera, et cetera.

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:27.960
Also Trisha Neely, Ed Walters,

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:29.670
Carlos Pinella of PWG,

00:13:29.670 --> 00:13:32.300
who has been instrumental
also in setting this up.

00:13:32.300 --> 00:13:34.710
Thank you from meeting
with us so many times

00:13:34.710 --> 00:13:36.450
as we've ventured over here

00:13:36.450 --> 00:13:37.610
to set this ceremony up.

00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:40.000
You've all been incredibly helpful.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:41.930
Petty officers second
class Michael (murmurs)

00:13:41.930 --> 00:13:43.440
and the Navy Band Quintet,

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:44.690
thank you for joining us.

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:48.530
Captain May, our masterful MC,

00:13:48.530 --> 00:13:51.563
who will keep this show moving
on or ahead of schedule.

00:13:52.900 --> 00:13:55.270
Those who provide never
ending support to ROTC,

00:13:55.270 --> 00:13:57.150
for the sake of time,

00:13:57.150 --> 00:13:58.080
I do see captain May,

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:00.710
I can see you from here
looking at your watch.

00:14:00.710 --> 00:14:02.140
I'll name but a few,

00:14:02.140 --> 00:14:04.130
recognizing those unnamed

00:14:04.130 --> 00:14:06.223
are just as important as those I do name.

00:14:07.190 --> 00:14:09.610
ROTC representatives
from our partner schools,

00:14:09.610 --> 00:14:12.420
professor Gary Rose of
Sacred Heart University,

00:14:12.420 --> 00:14:13.610
welcome.

00:14:13.610 --> 00:14:16.120
Jason Burke, director of
veterans and military affairs

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:18.080
for Quinnipiac University,

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:19.710
president Joe Bertolino,

00:14:19.710 --> 00:14:21.750
of Southern Connecticut State University,

00:14:21.750 --> 00:14:22.583
and of course, captain (murmurs)

00:14:22.583 --> 00:14:26.540
of the University of New
Haven Army ROTC program.

00:14:26.540 --> 00:14:27.950
From Yale faculty and staff,

00:14:27.950 --> 00:14:30.320
we have president Salovey present,

00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:32.890
secretary vice president Goff-Crews,

00:14:32.890 --> 00:14:34.670
Dean Chun, Dean (murmurs)

00:14:34.670 --> 00:14:36.600
Chaplain Kugler,

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:38.273
YVA president Adrian Bonenberger

00:14:38.273 --> 00:14:40.820
YVA president Shawn Flynn,

00:14:40.820 --> 00:14:43.670
also those representing
our awarding organizations

00:14:43.670 --> 00:14:45.790
who invest time and resources every year

00:14:45.790 --> 00:14:48.600
undertaking the important responsibility

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:51.050
of recognizing our students.

00:14:51.050 --> 00:14:53.630
Last but certainly most important,

00:14:53.630 --> 00:14:55.480
the family and friends of support network

00:14:55.480 --> 00:14:57.880
for our ROTC students.

00:14:57.880 --> 00:14:59.540
Colonel Granholm I thank you,

00:14:59.540 --> 00:15:01.300
We are proud of our
students and equally proud

00:15:01.300 --> 00:15:04.220
and appreciative of their supporting cast.

00:15:04.220 --> 00:15:06.969
So let's give a big round of
applause for all of those.

00:15:06.969 --> 00:15:09.552
(all clapping)

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:14.810
So please relax, enjoy the show

00:15:14.810 --> 00:15:16.433
and take plenty of photographs.

00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:20.410
At this time it is now
my distinct pleasure

00:15:20.410 --> 00:15:22.470
to introduce our host president

00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:24.840
Peter Salovey of Yale University.

00:15:24.840 --> 00:15:25.930
President Salovey has a long

00:15:25.930 --> 00:15:27.940
and distinguished career at Yale.

00:15:27.940 --> 00:15:30.050
He is the 23rd president
of Yale University

00:15:30.050 --> 00:15:32.470
and the Chris Argyris
Professor of Psychology,

00:15:32.470 --> 00:15:35.470
well known for his work on human behavior,

00:15:35.470 --> 00:15:37.570
human emotion and health behavior,

00:15:37.570 --> 00:15:38.700
especially his work,

00:15:38.700 --> 00:15:40.340
co-developing the world renowned framework

00:15:40.340 --> 00:15:42.650
called emotional intelligence.

00:15:42.650 --> 00:15:46.480
Additionally, he is an
uplifting and sincere leader

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:49.400
who always inspires and
encourages everyone around him.

00:15:49.400 --> 00:15:51.910
Whether they're faculty, staff, student,

00:15:51.910 --> 00:15:53.920
he inspires us to work together,

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:56.730
in strengthening our Yale
campus, our community,

00:15:56.730 --> 00:15:59.470
and our world through service to others.

00:15:59.470 --> 00:16:01.000
And these fine midshipmen and cadets

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:02.730
that you see standing before you

00:16:02.730 --> 00:16:07.090
all of whom upon graduation
will take a solemn oath

00:16:07.090 --> 00:16:10.240
of service to a higher
calling to their nation,

00:16:10.240 --> 00:16:12.380
have benefited from
president Salovey's caring,

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:14.970
compassionate and resolute leadership.

00:16:14.970 --> 00:16:17.090
So ladies and gentlemen please join me

00:16:17.090 --> 00:16:18.280
in a warm round of applause

00:16:18.280 --> 00:16:21.276
welcoming our host
president Peter Salovey.

00:16:21.276 --> 00:16:23.859
(all clapping)

00:16:25.586 --> 00:16:27.787
- Thank you so much, I appreciate that.

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:34.950
Thank you captain Withrow
for the kind introduction

00:16:35.930 --> 00:16:38.560
and for your service as commanding officer

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:43.450
of Yale's Naval Reserve
Officers Training Corps unit.

00:16:43.450 --> 00:16:46.970
I'd also like to recognize

00:16:46.970 --> 00:16:50.240
and thank Colonel Granholm,

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:51.640
for his leadership of

00:16:51.640 --> 00:16:55.220
Yale's Air Force Reserve
Officers Training Corps unit,

00:16:55.220 --> 00:16:57.460
as well as captain (murmurs)

00:16:57.460 --> 00:17:01.300
of the Army Reserve
Officers' Training Corps

00:17:01.300 --> 00:17:02.943
at the University of New Haven.

00:17:06.070 --> 00:17:07.460
So now more than ever

00:17:08.370 --> 00:17:12.860
we appreciate your
distinguished military careers

00:17:12.860 --> 00:17:16.600
and your commitment to advancing stability

00:17:16.600 --> 00:17:19.203
in the world through service.

00:17:20.920 --> 00:17:23.740
To all the parents and loved ones,

00:17:23.740 --> 00:17:25.710
active duty military members,

00:17:25.710 --> 00:17:28.570
veterans who are here with us today,

00:17:28.570 --> 00:17:30.170
I extend a warm greeting

00:17:30.170 --> 00:17:33.023
on behalf of my colleagues
across the University.

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:37.540
And I wish to welcome cadets, liaisons,

00:17:37.540 --> 00:17:40.840
and leaders from the
University of New Haven,

00:17:40.840 --> 00:17:43.750
Southern Connecticut State University,

00:17:43.750 --> 00:17:45.030
Quinnipiac University,

00:17:45.030 --> 00:17:47.023
and Sacred Heart University.

00:17:47.960 --> 00:17:49.583
We've all participated,

00:17:50.720 --> 00:17:53.160
we've all anticipated the day

00:17:53.160 --> 00:17:57.250
when public health
conditions would allow us

00:17:57.250 --> 00:18:01.163
to gather again for special
ceremonies like this one.

00:18:02.100 --> 00:18:06.330
And I'm so pleased that that day has come.

00:18:06.330 --> 00:18:07.163
I'm pleased that

00:18:07.163 --> 00:18:11.210
for the first time since 2019 we can honor

00:18:11.210 --> 00:18:13.020
the hard work and dedication,

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:15.773
your hard work, your dedication,

00:18:16.700 --> 00:18:20.333
of our ROTC students here in person.

00:18:21.620 --> 00:18:23.330
Our ability to celebrate today

00:18:23.330 --> 00:18:26.100
brings an extra measure of enthusiasm

00:18:27.190 --> 00:18:29.043
to a milestone day.

00:18:31.600 --> 00:18:34.730
The sense of service we mark
on this historic occasion

00:18:34.730 --> 00:18:38.710
dates to the founding of our institution

00:18:38.710 --> 00:18:39.853
and to the Republic.

00:18:41.140 --> 00:18:43.033
Let me give you a few examples,

00:18:44.010 --> 00:18:45.413
in 1701,

00:18:46.450 --> 00:18:49.800
Yale became the first American University

00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:52.500
to inscribe service to society

00:18:52.500 --> 00:18:56.143
explicitly in its founding documents.

00:18:57.940 --> 00:19:01.270
Scores of Yales have
served with distinction

00:19:01.270 --> 00:19:06.127
in every major conflict
since the revolutionary war.

00:19:08.387 --> 00:19:11.363
Yale Air Force ROTC,

00:19:12.310 --> 00:19:17.100
was one of the original
77 senior air ROTC units

00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:21.293
established by an order that
general Eisenhower signed.

00:19:23.510 --> 00:19:25.180
And Yale was also among

00:19:25.180 --> 00:19:26.960
the initial group of universities

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:29.970
to establish an ROTC program

00:19:29.970 --> 00:19:32.460
more than a century ago.

00:19:32.460 --> 00:19:36.440
Army ROTC in 1917,

00:19:36.440 --> 00:19:40.950
followed by Naval ROTC in 1926,

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:45.183
and Air Force ROTC in 1946.

00:19:47.280 --> 00:19:51.870
At the time Major General
Robert M. Danford predicted

00:19:51.870 --> 00:19:53.430
the program would become

00:19:53.430 --> 00:19:56.930
one of the many priceless heritages

00:19:56.930 --> 00:20:00.163
of future generations at Yale.

00:20:01.500 --> 00:20:03.910
And as I look out

00:20:05.260 --> 00:20:07.490
in this amphitheater

00:20:07.490 --> 00:20:09.993
I see that he was right.

00:20:11.490 --> 00:20:13.580
The 42 Air Force cadets,

00:20:13.580 --> 00:20:15.030
the 31 midshipmen,

00:20:15.030 --> 00:20:17.030
and the two Army cadets,

00:20:17.030 --> 00:20:21.160
currently participating
in Yale's ROTC program

00:20:21.160 --> 00:20:24.380
are carrying forward the proud legacy

00:20:25.280 --> 00:20:27.033
of those before them.

00:20:28.330 --> 00:20:33.330
This includes the valor
of nearly 30,000 Yales,

00:20:33.720 --> 00:20:37.080
who served in the first
and second world wars,

00:20:37.080 --> 00:20:39.623
Korea and Vietnam.

00:20:41.460 --> 00:20:43.230
And the spirit of sacrifice

00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:46.060
that links these military men and women

00:20:46.060 --> 00:20:51.057
is a hallmark of the
spirit of Yale itself.

00:20:53.640 --> 00:20:55.933
It is the desire to serve,

00:20:57.250 --> 00:21:00.000
to look beyond self-interest,

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:04.673
it is the desire to
contribute to the common good.

00:21:06.270 --> 00:21:10.700
And it is in short the desire
to improve the world today

00:21:11.610 --> 00:21:14.210
and for future generations.

00:21:14.210 --> 00:21:18.770
Indeed, that is the call
of the opening sentence

00:21:18.770 --> 00:21:20.083
of our mission statement.

00:21:21.960 --> 00:21:24.240
And this is the call,

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:28.253
the class of 2022 has answered.

00:21:30.360 --> 00:21:33.260
Your decision to serve our
country in the armed forces

00:21:33.260 --> 00:21:37.803
is especially critical during
this period of turbulence.

00:21:38.690 --> 00:21:41.890
We can see today

00:21:41.890 --> 00:21:43.890
the increasing presence of

00:21:43.890 --> 00:21:47.113
the spirit of division around the globe.

00:21:48.680 --> 00:21:49.803
Yet today,

00:21:51.716 --> 00:21:54.760
as we salute your accomplishments

00:21:54.760 --> 00:21:59.330
we can also feel the thriving spirit

00:21:59.330 --> 00:22:00.793
of the presence,

00:22:05.157 --> 00:22:07.643
and presence of the
opportunity for service.

00:22:11.790 --> 00:22:15.370
It's a spirit forged over centuries.

00:22:15.370 --> 00:22:17.210
Indeed the fabric of our nation

00:22:17.210 --> 00:22:20.500
is woven by the individuals
who have defended it

00:22:21.440 --> 00:22:23.783
for nearly 250 years.

00:22:25.070 --> 00:22:29.450
Their resilience, their
wisdom helped build a nation

00:22:29.450 --> 00:22:33.890
and their strength helped bind it together

00:22:33.890 --> 00:22:35.593
during difficult times.

00:22:38.060 --> 00:22:42.723
Their courage helped save a
world torn apart by war twice.

00:22:45.320 --> 00:22:48.400
Their determination has protected millions

00:22:48.400 --> 00:22:51.863
from a life of oppression and hardship.

00:22:53.690 --> 00:22:56.970
Their compassion has brought food,

00:22:56.970 --> 00:22:58.090
clothing,

00:22:58.090 --> 00:22:59.290
shelter,

00:22:59.290 --> 00:23:02.833
and medicine to those who need it most.

00:23:04.380 --> 00:23:06.620
And their unselfish motives

00:23:06.620 --> 00:23:09.097
in the face of adversity brought hope

00:23:10.370 --> 00:23:11.813
to those who are suffering.

00:23:14.710 --> 00:23:17.563
Throughout your time at Yale,

00:23:18.860 --> 00:23:23.330
I have seen the same sense of optimism,

00:23:23.330 --> 00:23:27.683
courage, and selflessness in you.

00:23:29.130 --> 00:23:32.090
And I know the class of 2022

00:23:32.090 --> 00:23:35.940
will manage to make us even more proud

00:23:35.940 --> 00:23:38.343
in the years ahead.

00:23:41.170 --> 00:23:44.820
So now, I'd like to congratulate

00:23:44.820 --> 00:23:47.260
all our students to be graduates

00:23:47.260 --> 00:23:50.180
and express my appreciation

00:23:50.180 --> 00:23:53.270
to all our midshipmen and cadets

00:23:53.270 --> 00:23:55.383
for their dedication to service,

00:23:56.436 --> 00:23:59.363
and their many contributions to Yale.

00:24:01.230 --> 00:24:05.100
You will soon be second
lieutenants and ensigns

00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:09.893
serving as America's
ambassadors around the world.

00:24:11.670 --> 00:24:14.810
You'll face challenges and opportunities

00:24:14.810 --> 00:24:17.620
in a rapidly changing global climate

00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:21.600
with incredible technological advances

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:23.750
that both connect people across

00:24:23.750 --> 00:24:25.780
national and cultural borders

00:24:26.620 --> 00:24:31.413
but also pose unpredictable
security risks.

00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:35.480
You'll experience shifts

00:24:35.480 --> 00:24:38.763
in political and environmental landscapes,

00:24:39.820 --> 00:24:44.730
yet regardless of what trials you may face

00:24:44.730 --> 00:24:47.320
I am confident that you

00:24:48.260 --> 00:24:50.130
like the generations of Yales

00:24:50.130 --> 00:24:53.540
who contributed to this country before you

00:24:54.830 --> 00:24:58.453
will lead and serve the greater good.

00:25:00.890 --> 00:25:04.853
We are so proud of what
you have accomplished here.

00:25:06.010 --> 00:25:07.430
We eagerly look forward

00:25:07.430 --> 00:25:11.730
to all that you will achieve in the future

00:25:11.730 --> 00:25:14.630
as you build on Yale's tradition

00:25:14.630 --> 00:25:19.630
of loyal service to
community and to country.

00:25:22.130 --> 00:25:25.263
I wanna thank everyone
for joining us today,

00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:30.210
and I hope you cadets and midshipmen

00:25:31.370 --> 00:25:34.410
will accept my most heartfelt

00:25:35.770 --> 00:25:39.380
and proud congratulations.

00:25:39.380 --> 00:25:40.523
Thank you very much.

00:25:41.841 --> 00:25:44.424
(all clapping)

