00:30:58.470 --> 00:31:00.730
The corporal Langdon Laws Ricketts award

00:31:00.730 --> 00:31:03.193
is presented by Mr. Adrian Bonenberger.

00:31:04.060 --> 00:31:07.640
Corporal Ricketts graduated
from Yale College in 1916,

00:31:07.640 --> 00:31:09.930
and was killed in action
during World War I,

00:31:09.930 --> 00:31:12.380
at Mont Blanc in 1918.

00:31:12.380 --> 00:31:13.620
A decorated Marine

00:31:13.620 --> 00:31:16.050
who was a recipient of the (murmurs)

00:31:16.050 --> 00:31:18.250
all accounts maintain
that Corporal Ricketts

00:31:18.250 --> 00:31:21.990
had a knack for maintaining
his wits in the face of chaos.

00:31:21.990 --> 00:31:24.400
Importantly, he was an enlisted man,

00:31:24.400 --> 00:31:26.810
a non-commissioned officer.

00:31:26.810 --> 00:31:29.430
NCOs are the backbone of the Marine Corps.

00:31:29.430 --> 00:31:31.040
More than any other service,

00:31:31.040 --> 00:31:33.610
the Marines place special
trust and confidence

00:31:33.610 --> 00:31:37.270
in the abilities of their
junior most enlisted leadership.

00:31:37.270 --> 00:31:39.490
It is this sort of
Marine that the recipient

00:31:39.490 --> 00:31:41.800
of the corporal Langdon
Laws Ricketts award

00:31:41.800 --> 00:31:43.660
will be called upon to lead shortly

00:31:43.660 --> 00:31:46.500
after their graduation from Yale college.

00:31:46.500 --> 00:31:50.138
